# Optional. Nothing here is needed to run AdmitPerf against a local engine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is read by every `admitperf` command, including the ones the dashboard
# runs for you. Anything already exported wins, so a one-off
# `HF_TOKEN=... admitperf infra up` still overrides the file.
#
# This is a superset of the lab's own env file plus AdmitPerf's. Every name
# below is read somewhere in this repository — the list was taken from the code
# rather than copied, and the file that reads each one is named. A variable the
# code does not read is worse than a missing one: it looks configured.
#
# The cluster reads its settings from the ENVIRONMENT, not from
# infra/config/cluster.yaml. The YAML records intent and generates manifests;
# these are what a running gateway actually sees. Where the two disagree, the
# environment wins and nothing warns you. See #15.


# ===========================================================================
# GPU box access                          infra/render.py, infra/setup/*.sh
# ===========================================================================
# Addresses and keys live HERE, not in infra/config/cluster.yaml, which is
# committed. An IP is a fact about today; the topology is not. Re-renting a box is
# an edit to this file rather than a commit, and a private key path is never
# committed at all.
#
# Resolved per host by name: host `gpu-a` reads LAMBDA_HOST_GPU_A, falling back to
# LAMBDA only when it is the ONLY host. With two boxes LAMBDA is ambiguous and is
# refused — applying one address to both would point every URL at one machine
# while the plan claimed two, which is a one-worker run reported as two.
#
# `render.py --plan` works with these unset. `--env` and bring-up refuse, naming
# the variable they want.
# TWO ADDRESSES PER HOST, because they are two different networks.
#
#   LAMBDA_HOST_n     PUBLIC. For SSH, rsync and bring-up, from your laptop.
#   LAMBDA_PRIVATE_n  PRIVATE. For worker URLs and the KV store, box to box.
#
# The provider firewalls the public interface down to port 22, so a worker URL
# built from a public address connects to nothing — and it fails as a timeout
# during a run rather than an error at configuration time. Measured on a Lambda
# A100 fleet: private 10.19.x.x answered in 1.55 ms; the same port on the public
# address was refused.
#
# Which variable each host reads is declared in infra/config/cluster.yaml, so the
# names below are a contract rather than a convention.
#
# LAMBDA_HOST_1=ubuntu@150.136.144.9
# LAMBDA_PRIVATE_1=10.19.81.206
# LAMBDA_HOST_2=ubuntu@150.136.38.150
# LAMBDA_PRIVATE_2=10.19.80.30
# LAMBDA_HOST_3=ubuntu@129.213.20.62
# LAMBDA_PRIVATE_3=10.19.95.9
# LAMBDA_HOST_4=ubuntu@129.213.151.97
# LAMBDA_PRIVATE_4=10.19.86.62
#
# Single-host shorthand, kept because the lab's scripts use it. Refused when there
# is more than one host, since it would then be ambiguous.
# LAMBDA=ubuntu@YOUR_LAMBDA_IP
# LAMBDA_SSH_KEY=$HOME/.ssh/YOUR_LAMBDA_KEY    # one key for every host
# LAMBDA_SSH_KEY_GPU_B=$HOME/.ssh/OTHER_KEY    # per-host override


# ===========================================================================
# Models and topology                     infra/gateway/serve.py, router/
# ===========================================================================
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING THE MODELS. Two traps live here, and both of them
# produce a run that looks fine and proves nothing.
#
# 1. TEXT_MODEL and VISION_MODEL being DIFFERENT, with LAB_SPLIT=capability,
#    makes the two workers non-interchangeable. Placement then becomes a
#    capability lookup — a vision request has exactly one destination — and the
#    admission question disappears, because there is no choice left to make.
#    The project's design (infra/config/cluster.yaml) calls for ONE model on
#    both workers so placement stays a load decision. The defaults below are
#    the lab's and do not match that. Pick one deliberately.
#
# 2. A 3B model will not fill KV. These defaults are 3B because the lab ran on
#    one small GPU. Admission never bites, every signal stays flat, and the run
#    reproduces experiments/half-capacity-headroom by construction. If you keep
#    them, you must also cap max_num_seqs by hand and declare the cap artificial.
#
# export TEXT_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct
# export VISION_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct
# export LOCAL_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct

# phase = prefill/decode disaggregation. capability = text worker + vision
# worker, which is trap 1 above.
# export LAB_TOPOLOGY=disaggregated
# export LAB_SPLIT=capability


# ===========================================================================
# Endpoints                               infra/gateway/, infra/router/
# ===========================================================================
# PREFILL_URLS, DECODE_URLS, LAB_TOPOLOGY, LAB_SPLIT, KV_BACKEND and LOCAL_MODEL
# are RENDERED by `infra.render --env` from the topology. Setting them by hand
# overrides the config silently, which is how a two-pool plan ends up serving
# from one. Prefer re-rendering.
# ===========================================================================
# export LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1
# export PREFILL_URLS=http://127.0.0.1:8000
# export DECODE_URLS=http://127.0.0.1:8001          # infra/setup/lambda_sliced.sh
# export ORCH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1

# Where the gateway itself binds.         infra/gateway/serve.py
# export SERVE_HOST=0.0.0.0
# export SERVE_PORT=8080


# ===========================================================================
# KV transport                            infra/router/kvbus.py
# ===========================================================================
# mooncake is the only backend that does anything. nccl and nixl are
# deliberate no-op stubs — four lines that return. Setting either makes hops
# vanish from Mooncake's /hops while requests still succeed, so ZERO HOPS IN
# THE DASHBOARD DOES NOT PROVE THE HOP DID NOT HAPPEN. Check this first.
# export KV_BACKEND=mooncake
# export MOONCAKE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:50051


# ===========================================================================
# Load generation                         NOT VENDORED YET — see #27
# ===========================================================================
# The lab's load generators (locustfile.py, crew_flood.py) live in its app/
# directory and were not copied into this repository. LOCUST_HOST is listed for
# completeness; nothing here reads it today.
# export LOCUST_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:8080


# ===========================================================================
# Overflow — paid capacity for when our own cluster refuses
#                                         infra/router/overflow.py
# ===========================================================================
# OVERFLOW_MAX_REQS is a LIFETIME counter on the gateway process, not a rate
# limit. After this many overflows the path turns itself off and requests fall
# back to the local refusal. That is a deliberate spend guard, and it is also a
# measurement hazard: a run can report "overflow did not help" when what
# actually happened is that overflow stopped being available. See #26.
#
# A 4B model answering in place of a 72B is a relief valve, not an equivalent
# service. Overflowed responses are not comparable to local ones and must not
# be pooled with them when reporting quality.
#
# Unrecognised names here are ignored silently and the gateway reports
# "overflow off" — a typo looks like a configuration choice.
# RENDERED FROM THE CONFIG, except the key. `python -m infra.render --env` emits
# OVERFLOW_BACKEND, OVERFLOW_BASE_URL, OVERFLOW_MODEL, OVERFLOW_MAX_REQS and
# OVERFLOW_MAX_TOKENS from infra/config/cluster.yaml, so they are not typed in two
# places where they would drift. Set them below only to override a rendered run.
#
# The key is the one secret in that block, and it belongs here:
# export OVERFLOW_API_KEY=                 # unset = overflow off
#
# export OVERFLOW_BACKEND=superlinked
# export OVERFLOW_BASE_URL=https://api.superlinked.com/v1
# export OVERFLOW_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B
# export OVERFLOW_MAX_REQS=20
# export OVERFLOW_MAX_TOKENS=64
# export OVERFLOW_MAX_REQS=20


# ===========================================================================
# Trace — one row per request             infra/gateway/serve.py
# ===========================================================================
# The report is generated from this file. It is also where the signal value
# behind each admission decision will be recorded (#11).
# export TRACE_PATH=traces/requests.jsonl


# ===========================================================================
# AdmitPerf's own settings
# ===========================================================================
# Override the config a command loads.    src/admitperf/core/config.py
# ADMITPERF_CONFIG=src/admitperf/experiments/my-run/config.yaml

# Gated weights only (Llama, Gemma). Forwarded to the engine at deploy time;
# never written into an image layer.
# HF_TOKEN=hf_xxx


